  "query_preview": "..Today through Thursday: A very active zonal jet \nstretching across the entire Pacific Ocean will be aimed at \nWashington and southern British Columbia over the next seven \ndays. The jet stream by the time it reaches the region will take\non a southwest-northeast orientation with multiple rounds of \nprecipitation. With the jet orientation and it being aimed at \nWashington and southern BC, the heaviest precipitation will be \nfocused over the Cascades, Olympic Mountains, Vancouver Island, \nand southern BC, with occasional sloshes of precipitation making\nit further east into Central and Eastern Washington and North \nIdaho with passing weather systems embedded within the jet.\n\nTiming of precipitation looks to pass through early Saturday\nmorning. The next round of precipitation is slated for Saturday\nNight into Sunday, then Monday Night through Wednesday, with \nyet another round of precipitation expected towards the end of \nnext week and into the weekend. The multiple rounds of \nprecipitation in the Cascades will be something to monitor and \nwith some uncertainty on the precise placement of atmospheric \nrivers next week there is uncertainty with rain amounts.\n\nTonight into early Saturday morning, the NBM is showing around\n0.33-0.50 inches of precipitation along the Cascade crest, \naround 0.15-0.50 inches in Central WA, and 0.25-1.00 inch across\nNE WA into the ID Panhandle. \n\nSaturday night into Sunday, the NBM is showing around 0.15-0.50\ninches of precipitation along the Cascade crest, around \n0.05-0.20 inches in Central WA, and 0.10-0.25 inches across NE \nWA into the ID Panhandle. \n\nMonday night into Wednesday, the NBM is showing around 0.20-1.25 \ninches of precipitation along the Cascade crest, around \n0.05-0.15 inches in Central WA, and 0.10-0.25 inches across NE \nWA into the ID Panhandle. \n\nBeyond Thursday, the NBM is showing another round of \nprecipitation towards the end of next week and into next \nweekend. However, there remains a lot of uncertainty as to the \nexact timing and amounts. \n\nIf the higher amounts verify for the Cascades there would be an\nincreased risk of debris flows or rockslides near burn scars in\nareas of steep terrain.\n\nThe active southwest jet will result in above normal temperatures\nthroughout the 7 day forecast. Highs will be mainly in the 50s,\nwith a few days possibly reaching the low 60s while in the warm\nsector of these passing systems (Saturday and Wednesday). The \npattern also had the potential for a few breezy days, including \nSunday with a weak cold front passage. This cold front will \nbriefly lower snow levels down to 3000 feet with NBM carrying a\n70 % chance of 2 inches or more of snow at Washington Pass, a \n50% chance of 4 inches and a 40% chance of 5 inches of snow. \n/42-JW",
